Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:38:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 50/50] feat(text): text keeps its lines, alignment, angle and
 font

A text layer rasterized its string as one line of bytes, centred on its
origin, at its origin's depth:

- "\n" broke no line, so a date set to stack day, month and year drew
  them side by side (9 layers carry a line break, more get one from a
  script). Lines now break, and line up by horizontalalign.
- Each byte was a character, so anything past ASCII drew as junk: a
  degree sign, an accented month, Japanese (21 layers). The text is read
  as UTF-8.
- The alignment says which point of the text sits on the origin, as it
  does for an image: a left-aligned label starts there and a right-aligned
  date ends there, so a widget's title stays at the left of its panel
  however long it is (105 of 237 text layers are not centred).
- The angle was ignored (22 layers are turned). It applies as an image's
  does.
- The origin's z went into the depth, and a clock at z 20 or a day name
  at z 94 fell outside the camera's range and never drew. Like an image,
  a text now leaves z out.
- `systemfont_consolas`, the font of 75 layers laying out calendars and
  tables in columns, fell back to a proportional face. Wallpaper
  Engine's monospaced and serif system fonts now get a monospaced or
  serif stand-in.

